    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change slots cleanly into the existing structure and reads exactly like code a maintainer of this repo would write:\n\n- **Right unit, right pattern.** `assertEmailDomainAllowed(Owner)` mirrors the established `assertPostcodeValid` / `assertRegistrationDateNotFuture` validation-helper convention: null-guard, extract, check, throw a domain-specific exception. It sits alongside its siblings and is invoked from `saveOwner` in the same `assertXxx(owner)` style.\n- **Constant placement matches the codebase idiom.** The class deliberately co-locates `static final` constants with their consuming method (`CITY_OWNER_LIMIT`, `DAILY_OWNER_LIMIT`, `ADDRESS_ABBREVIATIONS`), so putting `DISPOSABLE_EMAIL_DOMAINS` immediately above `assertEmailDomainAllowed` is consistent rather than an outlier.\n- **Exception + handler mirror existing ones.** `DisposableEmailDomainException` is a plain `RuntimeException` like the neighboring `DailyOwnerLimitException` / `CityOwnerLimitException`, and the new `@ExceptionHandler` is a faithful copy of the sibling handlers (same warn-logging shape, `detailBuild`, explicit `BAD_REQUEST`). Nothing is becoming a catch-all.\n- **No duplication or misplaced logic.** Call ordering (after `normalizeEmail`) is intentional and documented; the helper's own `toLowerCase(Locale.ROOT)` is a reasonable defensive touch, not a redundancy worth flagging. Imports (`Set`, `Locale`) are already present.\n\nNothing to change structurally.",
